  "reconstruction": {
    "text": "For every natural J>=2 and every real h>0, index nodes by Fin(J)={0,...,J-1} and define\n  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi)),\n  ell_j(X)=product_{k in Fin(J),k!=j} ((t_j-t_k)^(-1))(X-t_k),\n  Lambda=sup_{s in [0,h]} sum_{j in Fin(J)} |ell_j(s)|.\nThe quotient in the node definition uses real coercions, so its denominator is the real number J-1. The ell_j are real Lagrange basis polynomials evaluated at real arguments, and Lambda is the real supremum of the indicated interval image. Then\n  sum_{j in Fin(J)} |integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds| <= h Lambda.\nThe integrals use real Lebesgue measure with oriented endpoints; h>0 makes the interval order increasing. The absolute value is applied separately to each integral, followed by a finite sum. This theorem asserts precisely this total absolute integrated-weight bound, without adding signed normalization, individual positivity, an upper growth bound on Lambda, or quadrature exactness conclusions.",
    "text_sha256": "f1ea96ce77a1638551a2f39c70c357f15426cc88433c153570b835d65264ed5b",
